Step 1: Define the concept of supervised learning and unsupervised learning.

Supervised learning is when the input data is labeled with a set of labels. In this case, the labels are the values of a target variable. For example, in a classification task, the input data is a set of images and the target variable is the label of the object in the image.

Unsupervised learning, on the other hand, is when the input data is not labeled with a set of labels. In this case, the input data is raw data without any labels. This type of data is often referred to as "noisy" data.

